# Scientific Deep Drilling (Continental)

<center>
![](images/PK/PK4LM_20.jpg){style="height:300px;"}

_Kola Superdeep Borehole – deepest borehole (vertical depth), 12 262m_
</center>

This major engineering project spanning several decades drilled to a depth of more that 12km into the continental crust. This is just over 0.1% of the radius of the Earth.

Read more on [Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kola_Superdeep_Borehole).

## Scientific Deep Drilling (Oceanic)

<center>
![](images/PK/PK4LM_19.jpg){style="height:300px;"}

</center>

Much more significant engineering challenge to drill to significant depth at sea. Not least because the drill is deployed from multiple km above the sea-bed. The crust is thinner, though, and there is now a chance to drill through to the oceanic moho (i.e. reach the mantle).

  - [History of Ocean Drilling](https://www.earthdate.org/episodes/deep-ocean-drilling)
  - [The IODP project](https://iodp3.org/about/scientific-ocean-drilling-history/)
  - [Meng Xiang - drilling to the mantle (again)](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41561-025-01675-7)

