## Samples carried to Earth’s surface by erupting magmas

<center>
![](images/PK/PK4LM_21.jpg){style="height:260px;"}&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
![](images/PK/PK4LM_23.jpg){style="height:260px;"}
</center>

Magma ascending has plucked out material from the sides of the pipe and carried it up to the surface. We have to determine the conditions (and therefore depth) at which rocks came from by looking at the chemistry and mineralogy of the samples, and making some assumptions about the time taken to bring the material to the near-surface.

# Samples carried to Earth’s surface by obduction
<center>
![](images/PK/PK4LM_24.jpg){style="height:260px;"}&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
![](images/PK/PK4LM_25.png){style="height:260px;"}
</center>

**Obduction:** (the reverse of subduction)– when material from the downgoing plate is over-thrust onto the over-riding plate. Most common during continental collision / congested subduction.

[Wikipedia article](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Obduction) on obduction.

# Seismology

Study of Earthquakes and also *sound waves (shaking) in the earth caused by earthquakes*
<center>
![](images/PK/PK4LM_26.jpg){style="height:300px;"}&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
![](images/PK/PK4LM_27.jpg){style="height:260px;"}

</center>

Earthquakes are the release of stored (elastic, gravitational) energy through the sudden and rapid movement of two  adjacent blocks of rock, usually along a fault. Energy radiates away from the hypocentre (earthquakes location) as vibrations called seismic waves.

# Earthquake waves

<center>
![](images/PK/PK4LM_28.gif){style="height:250px;"}&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
![](images/PK/PK4LM_29.gif){style="height:250px;"}
</center>

**P wave / Primary wave / Pressure wave**

  * Compressional
  * Can travel through solids, liquids and gasses

**S wave / Secondary wave / Shear wave**

  * Shear displacements
  * Can travel through solids but not through liquids or gasses

# Earthquakes – Earth’s interior

<center>
![](images/PK/PK4LM_30.png){style="height:400px; float:right;"} &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
</center>

S\-waves do not travel through the liquid outer core – S shadow zone

P\-waves also exhibit a shadow zone – this is due to wave refraction as a result of increasing wave speed with depth

Core structure – wave paths and speeds consistent with an Fe \(iron\) liquid outer core and solid inner core that both contain significant amounts of Ni \(nickel\) and S \(sulfur\)

# Earthquake distribution / depth

<center>
<video autoplay controls width="75%">
    <source src="movies/GlobalEQCumulative.m4v"
            type="video/mp4">

    Sorry, your browser doesn't support embedded videos.
</video>
</center>

See how the distribution of Earthquakes is very localised — on Earth they occur mostly at *plate boundaries* (more on this later !) and are quite shallow (in the cool parts of the lithosphere).

In a few places, earthquakes are deeper. These are regions of *subduction* where plates are recycled into the Earth's interior. These are the only cool regions at depth.


# Marsquakes – the interior of Mars
<center>
![](images/PK/PK4LM_32.gif){style="height:400px;"}&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
</center>

Mars is one of the few places beyond Earth where we have been able to use seismological techniques to image the interior. Even so, with just one seismometer and very few ~~earth~~marsquakes, our ability to "image" is quite limited.

## Marsquakes – the interior of Mars

<center>
![](images/PK/PK4LM_33.png){style="height:250px;"}&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
![](images/PK/PK4LM_34.jpg){style="height:250px;"}&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
</center>

Data from the InSight lander recorded frequent marsquakes up to magnitude 4 that allowed researchers to probe the martian interior: a thick, fractured crust, mantle structure, with a liquid core that includes a sizeable fraction of light elements.

## Marsquakes – InSight Mission
<center>
![](images/PK/PK4LM_35.png){style="height:550px; float:right; padding:50px"}
</center>

InSight operated from 2018-2022, after which power from the solar panels had dropped to a fraction of the original value, largely due to accumulations of wind-blown dust.

We believe that the mantle is active and overturning (like Earth), but we can't image this because 1) not enough seismometers or marsquakes and 2) Mars does not have plate tectonic activity so the interior is much less variable than Earth's (more later !).

# Moonquakes

<center>
![](images/PK/PK4LM_38.jpg){style="height:250px;"}&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
</center>


Apollo missions (1969-1977). **ASLEP** (Apollo Lunar Surface Experiment Package) detected ~12000 seismic events including some meteorite impacts.

Hundreds of deep moonquakes and 28 shallow events. Deep quakes are probably driven by tidal stresses from the Earth that deform the entire moon. Shallow events included quite large (>5.5) moonquakes probably would be characterised as "tectonic" events. Other sources of seismic activity included meteorite impacts and artificial signals from lunar modules [see wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lunar_seismology).

More recent results from the Chandrayaan-3 (Indian) lander and a future Artemis experiment (SUBLIME) would deploy a seismic network of 3 component instruments.

## Moonquakes

<center>
![](images/PK/PK4LM_37.jpg){style="height:300px;"}&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
</center>

Demonstrated the existence of a solid inner-core and sharp core-mantle boundary. Potentially, there is a partially-molten layer at the base of the lunar mantle, though this might also be structure in the outermost part of the core.

The solid core has a radius of about 240 km and there could be a liquid outer core with a thickness of about 90 km. The possible partial melt layer sits above the liquid outer core and has a thickness of about 150 km. The lunar crust is roughly 40km in thickness.

# Venusquakes

<center>
![](images/VeneraLanderSurface.gif){style="height:300px;"}&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
</center>

Groza 2 instrument flew on Venera 13 and Venera 14

  * Microphone + single axis seismometer to measure vertical
      displacement of surface to micron precision
  * Spacecraft only survived *a couple of hours* – not much data / nothing of any real use (we'd need months of data)


