# Earth’s magnetic field may switch directions

![](images/PK/PK4LM_44.png)

The polarity of Earth’s magnetic field shifts every 0\.5 \- 1\.0 Myr

Magnetized minerals in rocks show the direction to Earth’s magnetic poles & provide a means of determining the latitude at which a rock cooled

Rocks formed over time record either ‘normal’ magnetic orientation \(like today\) or ‘reversed’\.

This is a global phenomenon\, & can be used to correlate rocks of the same age\.

<span style="color:#ff0000">Normal magnetic polarity</span>

![](images/PK/PK4LM_45.png)

# Why does Earth’s magnetic field switch?

_We’re not sure_

Perhaps core is simultaneously melting & freezing due to circulation of heat in the overlying rocky mantle

Perhaps tectonic plates descend to the core \(or just above\) & modify the flow of iron

_What will happen when it switches?_

Gradual reversal

Interference with telecom

Interference with magnetic based systems \(e\.g\. navigation\)

