# Relationships between planetary interiors, volcanism & types of crusts

# Mantle convection & density-driven flow

<span style="color:#ff0000">Increased density – lithosphere sinks</span>

# Mantle convection

![](images/PK/PK4LM_135.jpg)

![](images/PK/PK4LM_136.png)

_Ladbury_  _ 1999\, Phys\. Today\._

Surface expression of interior processes



  * Rising column of lower density mantle at a relatively stationary point
  * May come from as deep as the core\-mantle boundary
  * Rising material forms a magma


![](images/PK/PK4LM_137.png)

![](images/PK/PK4LM_138.png)

Hamblin & Christiansen \(2000\)

_Mantle Plume or ‘hot spot’_

![](images/PK/PK4LM_139.png)

![](images/PK/PK4LM_140.jpg)

<span style="color:#ff0000">Stationary needle</span>



  * When a plate moves over a plume\, a track of volcanoes results


![](images/PK/PK4LM_141.jpg)

# Mantle plumes & flood basalts

Flood basalts are large areas of new rock that form when a plume ‘stalls’

These large areas of basalt transported significant H2O\, CO2 & SO2 to the atmosphere

![](images/PK/PK4LM_142.jpg)

![](images/PK/PK4LM_143.png)

Tasa Graphic Arts

Wang et al\. \(2016\) Lithos

Distribution of Mantle Plume Traces



  * Plume traces are consistent within a plate
  * because they follow plate movement


![](images/PK/PK4LM_144.png)



  * Some plume traces “start at a large ‘flood basalt” area \(star\)


![](images/PK/PK4LM_145.jpg)

From Winter 2001

Imaging a mantle plume or ‘hot spot’



  * Although we see evidence for low density plumes & we can model their
  * behaviour\, they are difficult to image with seismic methods
  * Is their rel\. low density due to thermal or compositional effects?
  * Do they form at the core\-mantle boundary or within mantle?


